  {
    "episode_id": 78020,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78020.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "This is Frank. One of the things you mentioned, and I've listened to some of your stuff on CNBC, but one of the things I hear and I've read an article you wrote, and one of the things you hit on is the Democrats really struggle with their messaging. And so this is kind of a two-part question. So obviously, there's a larger problem that you pointed out a year ago. You wrote Narco, I think in April of 2019 about the Democrats losing out on rural voters and how it's gonna hurt them. And I really do believe that if it wasn't for the pandemic, that may have actually caused them in the general election. It just so happened that I believe that that event swung things to where it moved it in the Democrats' favor. But going forward, how can the Democratic Party regained the messaging that, hey, they won a party for the people because it's almost like they've lost that message. Even though the Republican Party does things that go against the will of their base, so to speak, sometimes, how do the Democrats regain their footing and have a messaging that they're for the national good of the people?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
