  {
    "episode_id": 78020,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78020.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 41,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it goes back to what Byron was saying about the challenges that I saw in rural America and you know I know that I recently you know when you look at the numbers you know Biden won by 7 million votes but it was only 45,000 votes in key states collectively made him the president so you can bemoan and say oh my goodness the system is totally wrapped up and you know we need to fix that And you're not going to get a constitutional amendment. So you're going to have to start learning how to win in states like mine again. And so I always say one country is my attempt to reintroduce the Democratic Party to rural America and rural America to the Democratic Party. And a lot of people think let it go. It doesn't matter. time after time after time, these close races, and certainly the Senate majority is dependent on being competitive in states like North and South Dakota, which usually, not that long ago, had four Democratic senators. And so what we're trying to do is, number one, figure out what the needs are. I think I have a pretty good handle on that coming from a town of 90 people. That's not 90,000, it's 90. I feel like like there needs to be this bridge between kind of the Democratic Party of the last 10 years, and what I hope the Democratic Party can be. When I talk about this, I tell people, guess what? What a cab driver in New York, maybe a first generation or maybe an immigrant coming to this country. What they want is no different than what a small business owner wants for America. They want the same things for America. But we have spent so much time dividing this country, regionally, ethnically, based on our religion, gender. I mean, everybody's looking for some kind of little You know, pigeonhole, we're Americans and we all want the same thing. And that's our country to be successful, our families to be successful, that we want to make sure that everybody has an opportunity and that opportunity is equal. And so when we focus on those things that we all mutually want America to be and we want for our families, we can bring this country back together. And that's really what one country is all about. That's why I called it one country, by the way. Everybody's saying, well, the world, I said, no. That's exactly what I don't want to be. I don't want to be this just about, you know, rural America and you better listen to us. I want to say we've got to listen to each other. If we want one country, we want our country governable and we want to be successful.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Biden won the popular vote by 7 million votes.",
        "confidence": 0.98
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker comes from a town of 90 people.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Biden won the presidency due to 45,000 votes in key states.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "One Country is an initiative to reconnect the Democratic Party with rural America.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "What a cab driver in New York and a small business owner in rural America both want is fundamentally similar.",
        "confidence": 0.94
