  {
    "episode_id": 78020,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78020.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "If I were still in the Senate, I would be putting together a whole group of people to sit down and say, okay, the legacy of what happened, you know, of the last four years culminating in January 6, is that our democracy for years has been on the honor system. You know, we're going to give you our tax returns. That's just a tradition. We're going to, you know, engage in the peaceful transfer of power. That's just the tradition. And so for years, we've nominated and elected and defeated people who have been gracious and defeat John Kerry. Al Gore, one of the most contentious elections ever, presiding over a process exactly like January 6 and doing it with grace. That's all based on the character of the people who hold office. One thing that we've learned is that when you elect somebody with no honor, with no sense of truthfulness, with only a selfish desire to be front row and center, the democracy doesn't function the way it should function. And so I think the lesson that we should take is that this democracy needs harder and faster rules. Right? We can't rely on honor of the person who is sitting in that chair any longer because we've seen what a dishonorable person can do to our democracy. So we need to strengthen institutions. And to me, you know, this idea of taking shortcuts. I didn't win, so I'm going to take a shortcut and bully someone down in Georgia or, you know, threaten someone's life in Arizona. you know, and then culminating in literally as you say you're the party of law and order. And I would tell you, I was on ABC on January 6, just watching the process before it hit the fan. And people said, were you surprised by the violence? I said, I was only surprised that the leadership, and I don't blame the Capitol Police, but the leadership was so ill-prepared. Because anyone who lived where I lived knew that this was a steaming pile, that this was going to explode on someone. And so I think that the lessons that we should take, if they do do a January 6th commission, it should also include reestablishing these traditions and norms and putting some teeth in it, like making sure that the IG, the Inspector Generals, have broader authority and can be fired only for cause and have a review process when somebody like Donald Trump comes in and fires the IG from the State Department. You know, we've got to look at all the things that happened, not just what happened on January 6th, but all of the norms that were broken that protect our democracy and how do we recover those norms, making the assumption now that we know that dishonorable people can sit in the White House, gratefully, the American public made a corrective action. But for four years and two impeachments, we had someone with no honor. with no sense of tradition, no sense of responsibility to this country.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
